{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Speed = distance ÷ time (m/s = m ÷ s)",
   "Acceleration = change in speed ÷ time (m/s² = m/s ÷ s). Change in speed = final − initial",
   "Distance-time graph: gradient = speed. Horizontal line = stationary.",
   "Velocity-time graph: gradient = acceleration; area under line = distance travelled"
  ],
  "title": "Speed, Acceleration and Motion Graphs",
  "content": "<p>Two equations and two graph types cover this lesson.</p><p><strong>Speed:</strong> average speed (m/s) = distance (m) ÷ time (s).</p><p><strong>Acceleration:</strong> a (m/s²) = change in speed (m/s) ÷ time (s). A negative value means deceleration.</p><p><strong>Distance-time graph:</strong> gradient = speed. Steeper = faster. Horizontal = stationary.</p><p><strong>Velocity-time graph:</strong> gradient = acceleration. Area under line = distance.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Using final speed instead of change in speed for acceleration</li><li>Confusing the two graph types — a horizontal line means different things on each</li><li>Not using the correct area formula for the shape (triangle = ½ × base × height)</li></ul>"
 },
